{
  // WHY THIS FILE IS ONE LINE OF CONFIG
  //
  // It used to be a "solution style" root: `"files": []` plus references to the app and node
  // projects. The effect is that `npx tsc --noEmit` — the command everybody actually reaches for —
  // compiles ZERO files and exits 0. Always. It cannot fail, so it proves nothing.
  //
  // That is not theoretical. On 2026-07-26 it reported "clean" while src/pages/AiAudit.tsx
  // referenced two undefined identifiers (ChevronRight, useMemo) and used a block-scoped variable
  // before its declaration. The page could not have rendered. Only `tsc -b` or an explicit
  // `-p tsconfig.app.json` would have caught it, and neither was wired to an npm script.
  //
  // So the root config now IS the app project: plain `npx tsc --noEmit` type-checks src/ and exits
  // non-zero when anything is broken. The named gate is `npm run typecheck`, and
  // `npm run typecheck:node` covers vite.config.ts, which stays in its own project because it
  // targets Node rather than the DOM.
  //
  // Options previously duplicated here (allowJs, noImplicitAny, noUnusedLocals,
  // noUnusedParameters, paths, skipLibCheck, strictNullChecks) are either already set in
  // tsconfig.app.json or implied by its "strict": false. allowJs was dropped because src/ contains
  // zero .js/.jsx files. Nothing was loosened, and the error set is byte-identical to
  // `tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.app.json`.
  // No "references" here any more. They were INVALID and always had been: tsconfig.node.json is
  // not composite and sets noEmit, so as soon as the root had real files to check, tsc rejected
  // them outright (TS6306 "must have setting composite: true", TS6310 "may not disable emit") and
  // aborted before type-checking a single line. `"files": []` was the only reason that never
  // surfaced — tsc does not validate references it is never asked to build. vite.config.ts is
  // covered by `npm run typecheck:node` instead.
  "extends": "./tsconfig.app.json"
}
